Map callback subprogram and nesting tests
========================================

Host reference-scope checks (executes extracted verifier routines):

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_callback_reference_scope.py

Build runtime tests with a recent clang:

  clang -O2 -g -target bpf -Itools/testing/selftests/bpf \
    -I/usr/include/$(gcc -dumpmachine) \
    -c tools/testing/selftests/bpf/map_callback_nested.c \
    -o /tmp/map_callback_nested.o
  clang -O2 -g -target bpf -Itools/testing/selftests/bpf \
    -I/usr/include/$(gcc -dumpmachine) \
    -c tools/testing/selftests/bpf/map_callback_reference.c \
    -o /tmp/map_callback_reference.o

Build the reference test again with -DBAD_RELEASE and -DLEAK_REFERENCE,
using different output filenames. Both variants must fail verification:
BAD_RELEASE tries to release the outer frame's reference through a
callback subprogram; LEAK_REFERENCE leaks a callback-owned reference
returned by a subprogram. The default reference test must load.

On the patched device, with a recent bpftool, load the nested object as
classifier, pin its maps, and run it once with a packet input. The result
map key 0 must contain the 64-bit integer 18. It covers:

  main -> outer callback -> ordinary subprogram -> inner callback
       -> ordinary subprogram returning a value outside [0,1]

Example (adapt temporary paths for Android):

  mkdir -p /sys/fs/bpf/callback_nested_maps
  bpftool prog load /tmp/map_callback_nested.o /sys/fs/bpf/callback_nested \
    type classifier pinmaps /sys/fs/bpf/callback_nested_maps
  printf '\0' > /tmp/callback_packet
  bpftool prog run pinned /sys/fs/bpf/callback_nested \
    data_in /tmp/callback_packet
  bpftool map dump pinned /sys/fs/bpf/callback_nested_maps/result

Keep the existing for_each_map_elem_bad_return and bad_ctx rejection tests.
Host compilation and reference-scope tests do not prove verifier acceptance,
JIT execution, recursion rejection, or concurrent map mutation on the device.

ARM64 tail calls now use a stack-backed shared counter. JIT-to-JIT calls
pass it as private AAPCS argument x5; the private for_each helper passes it
through C map iteration into each callback. Timer callbacks pass NULL and
start a fresh counter. Top-level BPF entries always start a fresh counter.
The public BPF helper prototype remains four arguments, so BPF bytecode
cannot supply the private kernel stack pointer.

Run the emitted-code regression on the host:

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_arm64_callback_tail.py

This executes production JIT prologue/tail/epilogue instructions with a
modeled C-helper ABI boundary. It checks repeated callbacks, ordinary
subprogram calls, shared counter exhaustion, invalid indices, empty slots,
and stack/register restoration. Real kernel concurrency and verifier load
acceptance still require device execution.

Build map_callback_tail_call.c with the same clang options as the other
objects. On the device load both programs with a recent bpftool, sharing
its maps, and put callback_tail_target's fd into targets[0]. Run the entry
once. result[0] must equal 33, preserving this tree's existing ARM64 limit
comparison (counter > MAX_TAIL_CALL_CNT). Reset result[0] and repeat: each
new top-level invocation must start a fresh counter. Remove targets[0]
and repeat: the helper must fall through and return without a tail target.

The cycle under test is:

  entry -> map helper -> callback -> tail target -> map helper -> callback

Also test ordinary-subprogram tail calls and nested callbacks. Stack depth
limits remain enforced; ARM64 accounts for saved native registers, the
counter slot, and retained C map-helper frames before permitting a tail
call from a nested frame. Some deeper combinations are intentionally
rejected rather than exceeding the kernel stack bound.
